WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.460
Some films are a must-see because of the part
they play in our collective cultural conversation.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.750
Watching them unlocks a world of references,
pub trivia answers, metaphors, and dinner

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.900
party icebreakers.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.530
But other films are required viewing because
of what they can teach us about love, hope,

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.090
despair, faith, family, and even—warm and
fuzzy alert—ourselves.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:23.820
Here are just a few movies you need to see...

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:24.820
Before Sunrise

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.460
Regardless of your own romantic track record,
it behooves you to catch Richard Linklater's

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.540
1995 romantic drama Before Sunrise before
you kick the bucket.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.090
It's perhaps the most accurate depiction of
two young people falling in love in cinematic

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.090
history.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.510
And since it takes place in as close to real-time
as most people would be willing to bear, it

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:48.180
affords you the privilege of being swept-up
into the ardor—in beautiful Vienna, Austria,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.180
no less.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.150
It's like you're a fly on the wall of a room
two impossibly charming people have built

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:57.739
out of pure conversation, on the first day
of a decades-long relationship.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:00:59.030
Dear Zachary

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:03.910
The 2008 documentary Dear Zachary is one of
the rawest, most heart-wrenching depictions

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:06.810
of what it means to be a family ever committed
to film.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:11.130
It's an emotionally devastating love letter
that also requires you to think critically

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.450
about the meaning of justice, friendship,
and parenting.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:19.280
Just one thing: make sure you watch it with
someone who has already seen you ugly cry,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.119
because tears are almost guaranteed.

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:23.229
Groundhog Day

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:28.780
Harold Ramis's 1993 comedy fantasy Groundhog
Day is mandatory viewing not only because

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.310
it's widely considered a comedy masterpiece
but also because it's been adopted, and rightfully

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.640
so, as a sort of Zen guide to self-improvement.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.060
"Ugh..."

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.039
"What?"

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:45.720
The fact that Bill Murray's spiritual makeover
is thoroughly non-denominational makes the

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.649
film, and its message, all the more universal.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:54.680
The change comes from within and is ultimately
motivated not by a desire to escape his time-prison

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.529
but to instead improve himself and improve
the lives of those around him.

00:01:58.529 --> 00:01:59.529
And it's funny to boot.

00:01:59.529 --> 00:02:01.189
How could you go wrong?

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:02.189
Spotlight

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:06.189
In the same family as "All the President's
Men" - Spotlight shows the power of investigative

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:11.349
journalism and how a free press can take down
the powerful; Spotlight is the film for you.

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:16.489
The film, which won the Oscar for Best Picture
in 2016, is a tense, dramatic thriller, even

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:21.600
though almost all of the action involves people
talking - debating, discussing, and thinking

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.769
about what the ultimate meaning and importance
of truth is.

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:26.349
"We gotta nail these scumbags.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:30.079
We gotta show people that nobody can get away
with this.

00:02:30.079 --> 00:02:32.900
Not a priest, or a Cardinal, or a freakin'
Pope!"

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.920
It's not always an easy film to watch, but
when you're done, chances are you'll want

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.599
to watch it again.

00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:40.129
O.J.: Made in America

00:02:40.129 --> 00:02:44.939
Dedicating almost eight hours of your life
to a 2016 documentary series about the O.J.

00:02:44.939 --> 00:02:49.379
Simpson trial may sound like a lot to ask,
especially to those who already sat through

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:53.150
a lifetime of media coverage about the trial
back in 1995.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:58.500
But O.J.: Made in America is less about the
trial itself and more about celebrity, misogyny,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:04.550
Los Angeles, identity, justice, race, and,
yes, the American Dream, including why that

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.489
dream is off-limits to large parts of the
population.

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:11.800
It's essential, not just to understanding
the O.J. trial, but to understanding our nation

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.250
as well.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.250
Election

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:19.359
Alexander Payne's 1999 comedy Election is
an almost perfect film about high school,

00:03:19.359 --> 00:03:21.530
politics, class, and infidelity.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:26.340
Set in Nebraska, the film captures the dreary
midwestern sadness and also takes stereotypical

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.510
characters, the jock, the trying-to-leave-the-closet
lesbian, and the overachiever and masterfully

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.109
humanizes them..

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:37.390
And of course, there's Reese Witherspoon's
masterful performance as the deceitful Tracy

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:38.390
Flick.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:42.760
"I don't know what you're referring to, but
maybe if certain older, wiser people hadn't

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.709
acted like such little babies and gotten so
mushy, then maybe everything would be OK."

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:51.629
Of course, almost everyone in the film is
also a pretty awful person, but hey.

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:53.599
This is high school, after all.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:56.700
Going Clear: Scientology and the Prison of
Belief

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.299
If you've ever wondered what the big deal
about Scientology is, 2015's Going Clear:

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:05.420
Scientology and the Prison of Belief is exactly
the documentary for you.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.340
But even if you don't think you're interested
in Scientology, the film is a fascinating

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.389
discourse on blind faith, manipulation, and
the willingness of crowds to follow charismatic

00:04:14.389 --> 00:04:16.800
leaders, despite all logic and reason.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.130
And that's a message for everyone, no matter
what your religion.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:21.440
Citizen Kane

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.230
No doubt some of you have avoided watching
1941's Citizen Kane simply because everyone

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.770
keeps telling you to see it.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:32.110
But there's a reason the Orson Welles classic
is considered by many film fans and historians

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.900
to be the most important, most influential,
and straight up best film of all time.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.191
Not only did it innovate all sorts of filmmaking
techniques that are still in use today, it

00:04:41.191 --> 00:04:46.031
did it in service to a dazzlingly well-told,
thoroughly contemporary story about a rich

00:04:46.031 --> 00:04:51.040
businessman who uses his money, personal charisma,
and influence over the media to become a powerful

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.040
politician.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:05:00.540
"I made no campaign promises, because until
a few weeks ago, I had no hope of being elected."

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.440
Some stories - and some movies - are simply
timeless.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.570
Thanks for watching!

